This is a textbook example of **coverage-gaming**: every production method is called (→ 95% line coverage), but almost nothing is actually verified. Here's the severity-ranked audit:

---

## 🔴 Critical — Assert-Free Tests (coverage without verification)

These tests execute code but never check outcomes. They'll pass even if the production logic is completely wrong.

### 1. `AddProduct_Works` (line 17)
Calls `AddProduct` but never verifies the product was stored.
```csharp
// Fix:
_catalog.AddProduct(product);
var stored = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNotNull(stored);
Assert.AreEqual("Widget", stored.Name);
Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, stored.Price);
```

### 2. `UpdatePrice_Works` (line 32)
Calls `UpdatePrice` but never checks the price actually changed. A no-op implementation would pass.
```csharp
// Fix:
_catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
var updated = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, updated!.Price);
Assert.AreEqual("Widget", updated.Name); // ensure other fields preserved
```

### 3. `RemoveProduct_Works` (line 38)
Calls `RemoveProduct` but never verifies the product is gone.
```csharp
// Fix:
_catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
```

### 4. `GetProductCount_Works` (line 63)
Calls `GetProductCount` on an **empty** catalog and discards the result. Two bugs hide here: no data setup and no assertion.
```csharp
// Fix:
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-002", "Gadget", 19.99m));
var count = _catalog.GetProductCount();
Assert.AreEqual(2, count);
```

### 5. `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (line 68)
Applies a 10% discount but never verifies the new prices. A bug in the `(1 - fraction)` math would slip right through.
```csharp
// Fix:
_catalog.ApplyBulkDiscount(0.10m);
Assert.AreEqual(8.991m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001")!.Price);
Assert.AreEqual(17.991m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-002")!.Price);
```

### 6. `ImportFromCsv_Works` (line 84)
Imports a CSV line but never checks a product was created with the correct fields.
```csharp
// Fix:
_catalog.ImportFromCsv("SKU-001,Widget,9.99");
var imported = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNotNull(imported);
Assert.AreEqual("Widget", imported.Name);
